PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Unanimous consensus has now been achieved across all panel members in support of Choice C (Impossibility to capture heterogeneity in bulk experiments). The central physical principle is that bulk calorimetric measurements inherently record an ensemble average over macroscopic populations, making it physically impossible to deconvolve microscopic conformational heterogeneity, intermediate states, and end-fraying pathways even under ideal experimental conditions. In contrast, assuming a zero heat capacity change is an operational modeling simplification rather than an inescapable physical limitation of bulk measurement under ideal conditions. My stance remains firmly fixed on Choice C as the correct and intended answer.
}

